@@ -59,3 +59,5 @@ Short records of choices that are costly to rediscover. Add one when a decision
 **D28. Reuse violations are static, per selected module, and declared by the module (`owns.imports`).** Every v1 module lists the library package prefixes it owns in `metadata.yaml` (JWT libraries for `authentication.jwt`, method-security packages for `authorization.rbac`, Envers and Javers for `audit.logging`, Spring Data REST for `crud.pagination`, CSV libraries for `export.csv`, Flyway for `deployment.docker`); the schema gained the optional `owns` object. `check_reuse` flags, in the Java the model wrote: an import under a selected module's owned prefix, a type declared with the simple name of a selected module's `provides.interface`, and a method returning `SecurityFilterChain` when `authentication.jwt` is selected. It works on comment- and string-stripped source so a mention in a comment is not a violation, and it reports file, line and the offending name. Modules that were not selected own nothing, so the same code is a violation at k=6 and not at k=0. The Assembler's own files are not checked (the Composer's are; those are what the metric is about). Reuse LOC uses a versioned counter (`loc-1`); `generate` writes `reuse_violations`, their details and `reuse{reused_loc, generated_loc, reuse_rate_loc}` into `run.json`. Trade-offs: it is name- and import-based, so a reimplementation under a new name (a hand-rolled JWT with the JDK's `javax.crypto`) is not caught, and a violation list is a lower bound; the limitations section must say so. `reuse_rate_tokens` is not implemented yet (needs the fixed tokenizer decision). Registry modules' metadata changed (the `owns` block only) before any benchmark run references them, which `module-spec.md` section 7 allows.
 
 **D29. Validation runs cheapest-first, stops at the first failing stage, and repair is the Composer with build errors.** Stages: install the vendored modules (`mvn -Dmaven.test.skip=true`), `mvn verify` on the app (`-Dmaven.test.failure.ignore=true`: compile errors fail the build, tests the model wrote need not pass, as `benchmark-design.md` section 6 says), `docker compose build`, then `docker compose up -d` and poll `http://localhost:8080/actuator/health` for `UP` for at most 120 s; `docker compose down -v` always runs. This order differs from the order the spec lists them in, because Maven reports `file:line` errors that make repair prompts precise and fail in seconds, while the image build repeats the same compile later. A project without `docker-compose.yml` fails immediately (an app with no compose file cannot meet `build_success`). `.env` is created from `.env.example` with a throwaway JWT secret that exists only for validation. Commands run through an injectable `CommandRunner`, so every stage and failure path is unit-tested with a scripted fake; the real runner is a thin `subprocess` wrapper. Build output becomes structured `BuildError`s (`java-compile` with project-relative path and line, `dependency-resolution`, `docker-build`, `startup-health`, `timeout`, `tool-missing`, `build-other`), which feed both the repair prompt and the failure-mode counts. Repair (`agents/repairer.py`) gets the error digest (capped), the full text of only the Composer files the errors name (capped), the list of everything else it wrote and the same path rules, so it can overwrite its own files but never an Assembler file. At most 2 iterations (`PROJECT_CONTEXT.md`); a rejected repair (bad path, malformed output) still consumes an iteration and its tokens are counted (`ComposeError` and `PlanError` now carry the usage of the call that produced them, so a failed call's cost is never lost). `first_pass_build` is recorded from the first validation before any repair; the reuse check runs on the final files, so a repair that introduces a violation is counted. `run.json` now has `build_success` (null when validation was not requested), `first_pass_build`, `repair_iterations`, `tokens.repair`, `validation_stages` and `errors`. Not verified: any real Maven, Docker or model run. The stages, the javac output format and the health check are tested against scripted output only; the first real run (`mortar generate --validate`) will likely expose format and timing issues.
+
+**D30. First-real-run hardening of the validation stage (found by code review, not by a run).** (1) `parse_maven` now turns backslashes into `/` before shortening a path to `backend/...`, and the javac regex accepts spaces in the path: on the owner's Windows machine javac prints `C:\Users\...\backend\src\...`, which previously stayed absolute, so the repair prompt never matched a blamed file to a Composer file and said "(no listed file is yours)". (2) `SubprocessRunner` resolves the executable with `shutil.which` first: on Windows `mvn` is `mvn.cmd` and a bare name is not found by `subprocess` without a shell, which would have reported `tool-missing` for a working install. A truly missing tool still returns 127. (3) The 120 s health deadline now starts after `docker compose up -d` returns, not before it, so a slow `up` no longer eats the startup budget. (4) A repair the Composer's path rules reject changes nothing on disk, so the pipeline skips the Maven and Docker rebuild that would repeat the same failure; the rejected attempt still costs an iteration and its tokens (D29 is unchanged on that point). Consequence: `run.json` for a run with a rejected repair shows fewer validations than iterations. Verified by 4 new tests in `tests/test_validation.py`; the real Windows run is still unverified.
